<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-boltzmann-ludwig" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Ludwig Boltzmann</title>
  <idx>Ludwig Boltzmann</idx>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Years:</alert> 1844-1906
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Citizenship:</alert> Austrian
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Core Contributions:</alert>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <xref ref="def-boltzmann-entropy" text="custom">Boltzmann entropy</xref>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li><p>Second Law of Thermodynamics</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <paragraphs xml:id="boltzmann-transitions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Before and After Boltzmann: From Mysterious Variable to Profound Understanding </title>
    <p>
      Before Boltzmann, <xref ref="sec-entropy-taximony" text="custom">entropy</xref> <m>S</m> was
      an esoteric-like thermodynamic quantity. He was able to frame this headache of a physical quantity
      as a <em>combinatorial statement</em> rooted in basic counting and leveraged the fact logarithms have the property
      that

      <md>
      \log{\Omega_A \times \Omega_B} = \log\Omega_A + \log\Omega_B \, .
      </md>

    </p>

  </paragraphs>

</subsection>